    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_5e1e42cd8cb3c4c9b522307b12877ca819704267a4cd2560e5521af630c7cd4e",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The integral estimate supplied by Lemma 1 of [3] is used to control the logarithmic measure of points in a multiplicative block [X,eX] where the remaining zero sum is too large. Choosing the cutoff T on the logarithmic scale and applying the resulting bad-set estimate on exponentially spaced blocks makes the block contributions summable, yielding an exceptional set of finite total logarithmic measure. This converts averaged control into rarity across scales, while still permitting exceptional points.",
