    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_da684e8b839c411a1635f0f0ab36a5a2a28f5853ecd5112a515e297e94629c3d",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "To begin the short-interval argument, the source applies (6) to the prime-counting discrepancy across an interval and introduces auxiliary zero sums through a cutoff T. It then changes the form of the resulting spectral expression into an integral contribution involving one auxiliary sum and an endpoint-difference contribution involving another, together with an error. The important decomposition is therefore integral versus endpoint-difference representation, not merely a generic low/high split; this unit does not yet bound either component or prove normal density.",
